  "result": "Short answer: neither number is justified, because \"more agents always give better results\" isn't true.\n\n- **Why it's tempting:** Splitting looks like a sports roster. More specialists should mean a better team.\n- **What's true:** Each extra agent is more model calls, and every handoff passes text that can lose detail.\n  - Cost climbs, which matters on your small budget.\n  - More hops means more places to go wrong.\n  - Agents that need the same context, like manual answers and booking, end up copying it around.\n- **When splitting pays off:**\n  - The work is truly independent, so it can run in parallel.\n  - One agent's context would get too big or cluttered.\n  - Parts need different tools or permissions.\n- **Where to start:** Your assistant does three jobs: manual Q&A, calendar check, and booking. Start with **one agent that has three tools**. Make fixed steps a plain workflow where the order never changes. Only split when you can show one agent failing.\n- **Soccer comparison:** A 4-3-3 beats 11 players all chasing the ball. Coordination costs more than extra bodies. The comparison breaks down because agents also cost money per touch.\n\nQuick question: for \"Is the laser cutter free Friday, and what's the max material thickness?\", which of your three jobs could run in parallel? Would splitting save you anything?",
